 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Memory Constraints:** Standard feed-forward methods fail at 2K+ resolutions due to memory (e.g., NoPoSplat requires >61 GB at 1024x576) [S1, unofficial]. LGTM uses <30 GB [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Holes in Rendering:** Without high-resolution supervision in Stage 1, predicted Gaussians may result in holes as they are not anti-aliased [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Geometric Stability:** In Stage 2, the primitive network must be trained with a reduced learning rate (0.1x) to prevent destabilizing the geometric foundation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Holes:** Without high-resolution supervision in Stage 1, predicted Gaussians may result in areas with holes when rendered at high resolution because they are not anti-aliased [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Standard feed-forward methods fail at 2K+ resolutions due to memory constraints (e.g., NoPoSplat fails at 2K/4K). LGTM handles 2K/4K with <30GB memory [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory:** LGTM trains successfully at 2K and 4K resolutions using under 30 GB of memory [S1, unofficial]. In contrast, NoPoSplat fails to train at 2K or higher due to memory constraints (OOM) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scalability:** Scaling from 512 to 4K requires 64× more Gaussians in standard methods, but LGTM scales efficiently at inference with only modest memory and time overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Enables high-fidelity 4K novel view synthesis, a capability previously intractable for feed-forward methods [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Decouples geometry and appearance; enables high-resolution rendering with significantly fewer primitives; no per-scene optimization required; versatile input support (monocular, posed/unposed two-view, multi-view) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** LGTM trains successfully at 2K and 4K resolutions using under 30 GB of memory. In contrast, NoPoSplat fails to train at 2K or higher due to Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors, reaching 61.85 GB at 1024x576 primitives [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** At inference, a 64x pixel increase adds only modest memory and time overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables feed-forward 4K novel view synthesis; decouples geometry and appearance; significantly fewer primitives than prior feed-forward methods; versatile (works with monocular, posed/unposed two-view, and multi-view inputs) [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** Requires a dual-network architecture; relies on high-resolution supervision during training to avoid holes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When high-resolution (4K) novel view synthesis is required without per-scene optimization, and memory is constrained [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-LGTM was published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026 by Yixing Lao, Xuyang Bai, Xiaoyang Wu, Nuoyuan Yan, Zixin Luo, Jean-Daniel Nahmias, Yanghai Tsin, Shiwei Li, and Hengshuang Zhao from HKU and Apple [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods which suffer from quadratic primitive growth and the per-scene optimization requirement of textured Gaussian methods [S1, unofficial].
+LGTM was published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026 by Yixing Lao, Xuyang Bai, Xiaoyang Wu, Nuoyuan Yan, Zixin Luo, Jean-Daniel Nahmias, Yanghai Tsin, Shiwei Li, and Hengshuang Zhao from HKU and Apple [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of prior textured Gaussian methods which required per-scene optimization, and feed-forward methods which suffered from quadratic primitive growth [S1, unofficial].
 
